course: dictionary
lesson: 15400
title: Spoken or written
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Spoken means said out loud, not written down. When you chat with a friend, you use spoken English. When you write a letter, you use written English. The same language can be both.
explain: Spoken is also the form of «speak» that goes after have, has, had or be. «I have spoken to my teacher.» «English is spoken here.» Read the words before spoken to find its helper word, such as have or is.
explain: Spoken can also describe how someone talks. «She is soft-spoken» means she talks in a quiet, gentle voice. «He is well-spoken» means he talks politely and clearly. Check your meaning by swapping in your own words: if the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Spoken means said out loud, not written.
fact: «Spoken English» is the English we say, in a conversation, a speech or a play.
fact: «Spoken» is the form of «speak» used after have, has, had or be, as in «I have spoken» and «English is spoken here».
fact: «Soft-spoken» means quiet and gentle when you talk.
fact: «Spoken for» means already taken, so someone else has it.
exercise: «We studied spoken English in class today, not written English.» What does spoken English mean? | *English that is said out loud | English that is sung in songs | English that is written down | English from a faraway island
exercise: «I have spoken to the head teacher about the trip.» Which word is the form of speak? | *Spoken | Trip | About | Head
exercise: «She is soft-spoken, so you must listen carefully.» What does soft-spoken mean? | Cannot talk at all | Talks very loudly | *Talks in a quiet, gentle voice | Sings every word
test: «The words were spoken, not written.» What does spoken mean in this sentence? | Drawn in colour | Written down | *Said out loud | Cut out of paper
test: «He is well-spoken.» What does well-spoken describe? | The colour of a coat | The weather | How a person walks | *How a person talks
exam: «The notes were written, but the words were spoken.» Which word describes the notes? | Spoken | Sung | Shouted | *Written
